# Which courses can be completed.
#
# THE PROBLEM (LeetCode 207 and 210, "Course Schedule"): n courses, each with
# a list of prerequisite courses; a course can be completed when all its
# prerequisites can. Which courses can be completed? A course on a cycle of
# prerequisites, or depending on one, never can.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition with a step budget, with the
# prelude's foldr, and, map and true (Hidden37Courses.coursesTerm in
# gezi/contest-corpora):
#
#     done    = Y (\self. \d. \c. d top (\p. foldr and true (map (self p) (prerequisites c))))
#     program = the choice of done n c c top over the courses c
#
# prerequisites c is a nested case on the unary numeral c (the program's own
# code), the Scott list of the numerals of c's prerequisites; the courses are
# let bound numerals, course i the successor of course i - 1; top is no answer
# (corpora/library/top); the budget n is the course count, written once as the
# successor of the last course. done n c is a Boolean: true when every
# prerequisite is done; applied to c and top, it yields c. Without the budget a
# course on a cycle reads ⊥ in DdLP96's reading (a choice is a meet and Y the
# least fixed point), the whole choice with it, and every closed βη normal
# form would be an answer. The directions it tests: C (choices on cyclic
# graphs: a course on a cycle yields nothing), B (merging by value: done c for a
# shared prerequisite is one value, reached along exponentially many paths in
# a ladder of diamonds), D (foldr with and partially applied, map with the
# recursive function passed as an argument), F (the empty set: a subtask whose
# every course is on or behind a cycle).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. Let C be the least set of courses such that a course belongs
# to C when all its prerequisites belong to C (the least fixed point of
# step(S) = {c | prerequisites(c) ⊆ S}, Hidden37Courses.completable). The
# program's answers (an answer of the program T being a closed βη normal form
# N with T ⊑_O N, Definition 14; the selection theorem holds of them, since
# they are the N with ◇(N + T = T)) are the numerals of C. PROOF, under
# Definition 14 (doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md): top ≃_O 0, so the program has
# the answers of the one with 0 in top's place. done is deterministic and its recursion terminates (the budget falls
# at every call), so done ⌜d⌝ c reduces to true, or to a term built on 0. By
# induction on d: done ⌜d⌝ c is true exactly when every chain of
# prerequisites from c is acyclic and shorter than d; a course on a cycle,
# or depending on one, has arbitrarily long chains and spends the budget. A
# course of C has chains of fewer than n courses, so with d = n, done n c is
# true for c ∈ C and is built on 0 otherwise. The only choice is the outer
# one over the courses, so by the selection theorem the answers are those of its
# members: done n c c 0 is the numeral of c when c ∈ C, and
# is built on 0, with no answer, otherwise.
# Hidden37CoursesReadingTest checks the untabled weak head against
# Hidden37Courses.completable on every prerequisite graph of three courses.
#
# THE SUBTASKS AND THE TRAPS: course_point/5 below, each with its shape. A
# trap is a near-miss program whose snapshot excludes an answer a setting may
# plausibly give: a setting that assumes a course done while it is
# computing it (the greatest fixed point) answers the courses on cycles, which
# are outside the snapshot.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHM: Kahn's topological sort, the counting of unmet
# prerequisites (A. B. Kahn, Topological sorting of large networks, CACM 1962,
# doi:10.1145/368996.369025); tabled evaluation keyed by the course, which
# completes a cycle with no answer (W. Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996,
# doi:10.1145/227595.227597).

#   THE SUBTASKS: course_point(?Point, ?Shape, ?Count, ?Seed, ?Kind), Kind
#   subtask or trap. Sized by a probe of 2026-09-30 (CPUs 0-7,
#   512 MB stack and table, the machine shared): tabling(weak_head) reads
#   the chain of 60 in 1765 ms, the ladder of 20 diamonds in 1743 ms, the
#   random 120 courses with cycles (98 completable) in 19856 ms, the trap of
#   cycles in 1506 ms (5 completable) and the trap ladder in 1497 ms (none
#   completable), each equal to its snapshot. Its time grows with about the
#   cube of the number of courses (a chain of 25, 50 and 100 in 169, 856
#   and 6409 ms): the course is a unary numeral found by a nested case, and
#   each key holds a numeral. The random 1000 courses (282 completable;
#   Kahn's reference 9 ms) are left to a setting
#   that indexes the courses natively and tables done by course.
#   - chain: course i requires i - 1;
#   - diamonds: a ladder of diamonds, each level two courses requiring the
#     level below and one course requiring both, so 2^levels paths reach the
#     bottom;
#   - random(Degree, BackEdges): each course requires up to Degree earlier
#     courses, then BackEdges prerequisites are added from an earlier course
#     to a later one, making cycles;
#   - trap_cycles: courses on self loops and short cycles among a chain;
#   - trap_ladder: a ladder of diamonds whose bottom is on a cycle of two.
#
# The shape is a constructor of hidden_37_courses/shape, chain, diamonds,
# random with its degree and back edges, trap_cycles or trap_ladder.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
